exec: "is it aligned" is not the same question as "does a MemChunk fit"

Allocate()'s consistency check tests both a chunk's address and its size
against MEMCHUNK_TOTAL, which is

    max(AROS_WORSTALIGN, sizeof(struct MemChunk))

On m68k that is 8, because a MemChunk is two longwords, while AROS_WORSTALIGN
is 4. So the check demands 8-byte alignment of an address on a target whose
own ABI promises 4, and refuses addresses that are correct by its own rules.

Not hypothetical. A program that builds a private MemHeader and aligns its
pool to AROS_WORSTALIGN -- ordinary Amiga practice -- is refused on its first
Allocate(). With a debug SysBase that is a dead-end alert; without one the
chunk walk silently breaks and the allocation merely fails, which is quieter
and equally wrong. On AmigaOS the same MemHeader works: Exec there does the
arithmetic and does not audit the caller's alignment.

Bellatrix's point is to be compatible with the Amiga, so the answer to a
correct third-party binary cannot be "recompile it". The address is now
checked against AROS_WORSTALIGN, which is the alignment this target actually
promises; the size stays checked against MEMCHUNK_TOTAL, because that is what
guarantees a split always leaves room behind for a MemChunk -- a real
invariant rather than an ABI assumption.

Corruption detection is not weakened in any way that matters: odd addresses,
misaligned ones and sizes that are not a whole number of chunks are all still
caught. What is no longer caught is a caller obeying the documented rule.

The reporter in memory.c is split the same way, so it stops calling an
address unaligned that the allocator now accepts.

See AI_context/issues/ISSUE-0052.md, which also lists this as the fourth
appearance of the same 4-versus-8 seam in this port.

--- a/rom/exec/memory_nommu.c
+++ b/rom/exec/memory_nommu.c
@@ -168,8 +168,40 @@
